 ## Background
-Procedural noise generation has evolved from CPU-based texture baking to GPU-accelerated real-time evaluation. Early implementations focused on memory efficiency, using compact data structures like node pools and brick pools to minimize VRAM usage [S1, unofficial]. Modern systems often integrate noise generation directly into rendering engines or game engines (e.g., Unity, custom WebGPU raymarchers) to support dynamic, destructible, or streaming volumes [S2, unofficial][S3, unofficial][S4].
+Procedural noise generation has evolved from CPU-based texture synthesis to GPU-accelerated on-the-fly computation. Early implementations relied on CPU generation and memory transfer, but modern techniques leverage GPU shaders for real-time generation to save memory [S1, unofficial]. Specific implementations like the "Two-rate field" generation optimize large-scale voxel planets by separating global and local octave evaluation [S3, unofficial]. Integrated engines like Buto provide built-in systems to bake these procedural fields into 3D textures for volume rendering [S4].
 
